{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Joseph swore the children of Israel.</b> The verse should properly have said 'and Joseph swore his brothers,' but this teaches that he swore every single one who is of the children of Israel, even those yet to be born, and said to them: ״פקד יפקד אלהים אתכם״. And therefore Moses our master, peace be upon him, took pains to bring up his bones and was scrupulous about Joseph's oath, as it is written (Exodus 13): ״ויקח משה את עצמות יוסף עמו כי השבע השביע את בני ישראל״ — the verse explains that because of the oath he swore the children of Israel, he [Moses] took them himself, as though Joseph had sworn him specifically." "<b>And behold.</b> Joseph merited that Moses our master, peace be upon him, would bring up his bones באוכלוסא — that is, as it is written (Joshua 24): ״ואת עצמות יוסף אשר העלו בני ישראל ממצרים קברו בשכם״ — for it is well known that the rule of a funeral procession for a righteous person's burial is באוכלוסא, that is, sixty myriads, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'as its giving, so too its taking — just as its giving was with sixty myriads, so too its taking was with sixty myriads.'" "<b>And you shall bring up my bones from here,</b> from this place. <b>Some have explained</b> that each and every tribe brought up the bones of its [own] tribesmen — so that, for example, you would say the tribe of Reuben brought up the bones of Reuben their father, and the tribe of Judah brought up the bones of Judah their father, and likewise each and every tribe."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:08.049033367Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}